power: supply: rk817: track the real pack capacity

Two numbers drive what the gauge shows, and both are off on this device.

The full charge capacity comes from NVRAM and says 3853 mAh. A full discharge
measured 3769 mAh from unplugging down to a counter of zero, plus 375 mAh the
counter ran past zero before the pack was empty, so the cell holds about
4144 mAh. That capacity is the divisor every percentage is worked out from:
too small and the gauge reaches 0% while the pack still runs for another 39
minutes, which is what happens today.

Separately the counter is only re-based on a completed charge. The charger
holds 4.3V against a pack the driver considers full at 4.136V, so the counter
runs past the capacity and the reported level stays at 100% until the excess
has been used up.

Let a board state its capacity with a device tree property on the charger
node, read in probe where the other rockchip properties are read. Only
miniloong sets it, every other board keeps the NVRAM value and its own
behaviour. The sanity check on the NVRAM figure is widened to 10% over the
design capacity so a value this driver learned is not thrown away on the next
boot.

Learn from a counter that runs below zero while discharging: the amount below
zero is what the capacity is short by, because the gauge counted that much
charge out of a pack that still had it. Three repeats below 3.5V are required
so one bad read cannot move it, and one correction per discharge session so an
hour at empty does not collect it over and over.

Only one direction is measurable. A capacity that is too small shows up as a
counter below zero. A capacity that is too large shows up as the pack dying
with a number still on screen, and by then there is nothing left to read. So
this pulls a low figure up and never down, which is the direction that matters
here: the factory wrote 3853 mAh for a cell holding 4144.

Also re-base an over-full counter while discharging instead of clamping it,
ignore readings above 2x the capacity, and warn when the PMIC does not take
the write.

Signed-off-by: agugu2000 <agugu2000@users.noreply.github.com>

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/rk817_charger.c b/drivers/power/supply/rk817_charger.c
index 9436c6b..c2256f0 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/rk817_charger.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/rk817_charger.c
@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@
 #include <linux/unaligned.h>
 #include <linux/devm-helpers.h>
 #include <linux/mfd/rk808.h>
+#include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/irq.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
@@ -87,6 +88,13 @@ struct rk817_charger {
 	 */
 	bool soc_cal;
 
+	/* An over-full counter was re-based during this discharge session. */
+	bool overfull_rebased;
+
+	/* Consecutive below-zero readings, and whether this session learned. */
+	int empty_strikes;
+	bool empty_learned;
+
 	/* Implementation specific immutable properties from device tree */
 	int res_div;
 	int sleep_enter_current_ua;
@@ -108,6 +116,35 @@ struct rk817_charger {
 	struct delayed_work work;
 };
 
+/*
+ * The RK817 coulomb counter is only re-based when the PMIC is power cycled.
+ * The charger, however, keeps pushing charge into the battery past the point
+ * this driver considers full, so the counter can end up above the full charge
+ * capacity. Clamping it there reports 100% until that excess has been consumed
+ * again, which makes the gauge look stuck at 100% right after unplugging and
+ * then drop quickly.
+ *
+ * When enabled, an over-full counter seen while discharging is re-based to the
+ * full charge capacity, so the reported capacity starts dropping as soon as the
+ * charger is unplugged. Runtime kill switch:
+ *
+ *   echo 0 > /sys/module/rk817_charger/parameters/overfull_recalib
+ */
+static bool overfull_recalib = true;
+module_param(overfull_recalib, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(overfull_recalib,
+		 "Re-base an over-full coulomb counter instead of pinning capacity at 100%");
+
+/*
+ * Learning the pack capacity from a counter that ran past empty. Kill switch:
+ *
+ *   echo 0 > /sys/module/rk817_charger/parameters/empty_learn
+ */
+static bool empty_learn = true;
+module_param(empty_learn, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(empty_learn,
+		 "Raise the full charge capacity when the counter runs past empty");
+
 /* ADC coefficients extracted from BSP kernel */
 #define ADC_TO_CURRENT(adc_value, res_div)	\
 	(adc_value * 172 / res_div)
@@ -332,6 +369,159 @@ static int rk817_bat_calib_cap(struct rk817_charger *charger)
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Re-base an over-full coulomb counter to the full charge capacity.
+ *
+ * Only acts while discharging: that is where an over-full counter turns into a
+ * stuck 100% reading, and it also avoids touching the counter while the charger
+ * is still pushing charge in. The current reading used here is the one from the
+ * previous update, which is good enough to tell charging and discharging apart.
+ *
+ * Only one re-base is done per discharge session. If the counter still reads
+ * over-full afterwards, the new base has not reached the PMIC yet, and redoing
+ * the same register write every update would only repeat it and its log line
+ * until the counter catches up.
+ *
+ * A reading above 2x fcc is treated as a failed register read and ignored: no
+ * cell that fits in this device holds that much, so acting on it would wipe a
+ * counter that is still valid. The largest over-full reading measured on a
+ * real pack is 1.71x fcc, well under the bound, so the check only catches
+ * values no cell can produce and never a genuine over-full counter.
+ *
+ * Limits: the current sample is one update old, so an unplug is acted on up to
+ * eight seconds late and a plug in can still look like discharging once. And
+ * when the PMIC does not take the write, nothing retries it until the next
+ * discharge.
+ */
+static void rk817_recalib_overfull(struct rk817_charger *charger)
+{
+	struct rk808 *rk808 = charger->rk808;
+	int fcc_uah = charger->fcc_mah * 1000;
+	int over_uah = charger->charge_now_uah;
+	u8 bulk_reg[4];
+	int charge_now_adc;
+
+	if (!overfull_recalib)
+		return;
+
+	if (!charger->battery_present)
+		return;
+
+	if (charger->cur_avg_ua >= 0) {
+		/* Charging or idle, the next discharge may re-base again. */
+		charger->overfull_rebased = false;
+		return;
+	}
+
+	if (charger->overfull_rebased)
+		return;
+
+	if (over_uah <= fcc_uah)
+		return;
+
+	if (over_uah > fcc_uah * 2)
+		return;
+
+	charge_now_adc = CHARGE_TO_ADC(charger->fcc_mah, charger->res_div);
+	put_unaligned_be32(charge_now_adc, bulk_reg);
+	if (regmap_bulk_write(rk808->regmap, RK817_GAS_GAUGE_Q_INIT_H3,
+			      bulk_reg, 4) < 0) {
+		dev_warn_ratelimited(charger->dev,
+				     "could not re-base the counter\n");
+		return;
+	}
+
+	charger->charge_now_uah = fcc_uah;
+	charger->overfull_rebased = true;
+
+	dev_info(charger->dev,
+		 "counter %d uAh above full capacity %d uAh, re-based\n",
+		 over_uah, fcc_uah);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Learn the real capacity from a pack that keeps running past an empty
+ * counter.
+ *
+ * The counter only goes below zero when the gauge has counted more charge out
+ * than the capacity it was given, so the pack still holds charge at that
+ * point. The amount below zero is what the capacity is short by as of that
+ * moment. It under-reads a little, because the pack is cut off slightly above
+ * real empty, never over-reads. Adding it back makes the next charge start
+ * from a closer figure. That is the whole calculation, no curve, no model.
+ *
+ * Three guards keep a bad reading from moving the capacity:
+ *  - the pack has to be discharging and already below 3.5V, so a negative
+ *    value from a failed register read at 4V is ignored;
+ *  - the reading has to repeat three times, one bogus sample is not enough;
+ *  - one correction per discharge session, so an hour below zero does not
+ *    collect the same amount over and over.
+ *
+ * Only one direction is measurable. A capacity that is too small shows up as a
+ * counter below zero. A capacity that is too large shows up as the pack dying
+ * with a number still on screen, and by then there is nothing left to read, so
+ * this pulls a low figure up and never down. That is the direction that
+ * matters for a fresh pack: the factory wrote 3853 mAh for a cell holding
+ * 4144, and the level reached 0% with seven percent still in it.
+ *
+ * The result is capped at 10% over the design capacity. Past that it is not
+ * a pack that holds more than its label, it is a gauge that has lost count.
+ */
+static void rk817_learn_empty(struct rk817_charger *charger, int adc)
+{
+	u32 below;
+	int deficit_mah;
+	int fcc_max_mah;
+
+	if (!empty_learn)
+		return;
+
+	if (!charger->battery_present)
+		return;
+
+	if (charger->empty_learned)
+		return;
+
+	/* Last update's current, same as the re-base above uses. */
+	/*
+	 * Charging or idle. Only the strike count is dropped here; the learned
+	 * flag survives until the counter climbs back above zero, so a load
+	 * that happens to read zero mid-discharge cannot make the same
+	 * correction land twice.
+	 */
+	if (charger->cur_avg_ua >= 0) {
+		charger->empty_strikes = 0;
+		return;
+	}
+
+	if (charger->volt_avg_uv > 3500000) {
+		charger->empty_strikes = 0;
+		return;
+	}
+
+	if (++charger->empty_strikes < 3)
+		return;
+
+	charger->empty_strikes = 0;
+
+	/* adc is negative, this is its magnitude without the signed negation. */
+	below = 0u - (u32)adc;
+	deficit_mah = below / 3600 * 172 / charger->res_div / 1000;
+	if (!deficit_mah)
+		return;
+
+	fcc_max_mah = charger->bat_charge_full_design_uah / 1000 * 11 / 10;
+	if (charger->fcc_mah + deficit_mah > fcc_max_mah)
+		return;
+
+	charger->empty_learned = true;
+	charger->fcc_mah += deficit_mah;
+
+	dev_info(charger->dev,
+		 "counter ran %d mAh below empty, capacity is now %d mAh\n",
+		 deficit_mah, charger->fcc_mah);
+}
+
 static void rk817_read_props(struct rk817_charger *charger)
 {
 	int tmp, reg;
@@ -357,9 +547,28 @@ static void rk817_read_props(struct rk817_charger *charger)
 	regmap_bulk_read(charger->rk808->regmap, RK817_GAS_GAUGE_Q_PRES_H3,
 			 bulk_reg, 4);
 	tmp = get_unaligned_be32(bulk_reg);
-	charger->charge_now_uah = ADC_TO_CHARGE_UAH(tmp, charger->res_div);
-	if (charger->charge_now_uah < 0)
+	if (tmp < 0) {
+		/*
+		 * The counter wrapped below zero, so the pack outlasted it. Report
+		 * empty and hand the raw length below zero to the learning code.
+		 */
 		charger->charge_now_uah = 0;
+		rk817_learn_empty(charger, tmp);
+	} else {
+		charger->charge_now_uah = ADC_TO_CHARGE_UAH(tmp, charger->res_div);
+		if (charger->charge_now_uah < 0)
+			charger->charge_now_uah = 0;
+		/*
+		 * Back above zero: either still charged or on the way up. Either
+		 * way the next run below zero is a fresh correction.
+		 */
+		charger->empty_strikes = 0;
+		charger->empty_learned = false;
+	}
+
+	/* Re-base an over-full counter before it gets clamped below. */
+	rk817_recalib_overfull(charger);
+
 	if (charger->charge_now_uah > charger->fcc_mah * 1000)
 		charger->charge_now_uah = charger->fcc_mah * 1000;
 
@@ -718,8 +927,14 @@ static int rk817_read_battery_nvram_values(struct rk817_charger *charger)
 	 * practicality purposes. Also check if the value is too large and
 	 * correct it.
 	 */
+	/*
+	 * The upper bound allows for a figure the driver learned from a pack
+	 * that outlasted the counter, which is above the design capacity. A
+	 * value past 10% over design is not a bigger cell, it is a bad register.
+	 */
 	if ((charger->fcc_mah < 500) ||
-	   ((charger->fcc_mah * 1000) > charger->bat_charge_full_design_uah)) {
+	   ((charger->fcc_mah * 1000) >
+	    charger->bat_charge_full_design_uah / 10 * 11)) {
 		dev_info(charger->dev,
 			 "Invalid NVRAM max charge, setting to %u uAH\n",
 			 charger->bat_charge_full_design_uah);
@@ -1066,6 +1281,7 @@ static int rk817_charger_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	struct power_supply_config pscfg = {};
 	int plugin_irq, plugout_irq;
 	int of_value;
+	u32 fcc_uah = 0;
 	int ret;
 
 	node = of_get_child_by_name(dev->parent->of_node, "charger");
@@ -1172,6 +1388,26 @@ static int rk817_charger_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * A board can state what its cell actually holds. The NVRAM figure is
+	 * what the factory wrote and can be well short of the real capacity;
+	 * every percentage is divided by it, so a figure that is too small makes
+	 * the gauge reach 0% with charge still in the pack. Read it from the
+	 * charger node, the same one the resistor and sleep currents come from.
+	 * Boards that do not set the property keep the NVRAM figure untouched.
+	 */
+	if (!of_property_read_u32(node,
+				  "rockchip,charge-full-microamp-hours",
+				  &fcc_uah)) {
+		if (fcc_uah >= 500000 &&
+		    fcc_uah <= charger->bat_charge_full_design_uah / 10 * 11)
+			charger->fcc_mah = fcc_uah / 1000;
+		else
+			dev_warn(dev,
+				 "ignoring rockchip,charge-full-microamp-hours = %u\n",
+				 fcc_uah);
+	}
+
 	power_supply_put_battery_info(charger->bat_ps, bat_info);
 
 	plugin_irq = platform_get_irq(pdev, 0);
